WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.969
-Gwen, it is so good to see you.
-Whoo!

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.004
Thank you so much
for coming back to the show.

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:07.840
You were with me when I
really, really needed you,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.644
when we first were doing the
shows from the home shows

00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:13.146
and we were on Zoom.

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.648
And do you remember shaving --

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:18.785
You shaved my initials into
Blake Shelton's head.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:22.021
-One of my favorite moments of
my life was actually doing that.

00:00:22.021 --> 00:00:23.756
Two of my favorite human beings.

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:27.861
I get to create art that was --
It wasn't very lasting, though.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:29.329
It did grow out.
-Yeah, I figured.

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.964
-But that was amazing.
-Yeah, I figured.

00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:35.602
-I never thought that I would be
with someone that a mullet ever.

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:37.770
-I think he pulled it off.
He looked good.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.205
-Yeah. He looked good.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:42.008
I was sad when he wanted to hack
it off.

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:43.943
I was really upset about it.

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:46.379
-I don't know how this is true,
but I was researching

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:47.580
some things
before this interview

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.916
and I saw that it's the
25th anniversary of

00:00:50.916 --> 00:00:53.585
"Tragic Kingdom," of No Doubt's
"Tragic Kingdom."

00:00:53.585 --> 00:00:55.521
25 -- is that --
-It's still crazy.

00:00:55.521 --> 00:00:58.557
-How is that possible?
-It's true. I don't know.

00:00:58.557 --> 00:01:00.960
And I think --
Last week, I started

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.463
posting and looking
at all these people posting,

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:05.164
and it was really emotional,

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:07.901
like, in a
crazy way I wasn't expecting,

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.068
'cause I don't really do --

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:10.736
Like, I don't really care
about anniversaries,

00:01:10.736 --> 00:01:12.771
but you start to actually
think about it,

00:01:12.771 --> 00:01:15.708
especially this year,
with everything being so crazy

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:18.044
and just imagining all
the people

00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:19.879
that have come up to me saying,
you know,

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:21.180
"That was my favorite record."

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.882
And it's such a weird record.

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:25.918
Like, those songs --
like, they don't fit in anywhere

00:01:25.918 --> 00:01:29.488
in this world, but, yet, so many
people connected to them, and --

00:01:29.488 --> 00:01:31.024
-Oh, it was the biggest thing
in the world.

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:34.660
I mean, I was out in L.A.
when it was released

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.762
and I was doing stand-up.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:37.163
And, you know --
And, like, you spend

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:38.531
a lot of time driving in L.A.

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:40.066
And you were on the radio
all the time.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:44.370
It was so -- I have nothing but
great memories from that album.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.573
But, I mean, when you made
the album cover, did you think,

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:49.475
"This album is going to be big"
or "It's going to be a hit"

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.277
or "We're going to be famous"?

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:53.179
-No. We were just --
I mean, we were trying to

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:54.780
make that record for so long.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.815
We were in our band for
nine years

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:57.717
before that record came out.

00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:00.787
So we never --
Like, we put a fake record out

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:02.321
before that, 'cause
the record company wouldn't

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:03.556
put our record out,
so we were like,

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.525
"Oh, we're just going to put
this record out."

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:08.194
And we, like -- I did, like,
the collage for it.

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.396
I went to Kinko's and I Xeroxed
all these pictures of us,

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:11.463
and we put this
record out called

00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:13.533
"The Beacon Street Collection."

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:14.800
Just kind of like,
"Well, if you're not going to

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.736
put one out,
we'll put this really bad one."

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:20.439
No. The record we put out was,
like, kind of all the songs

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:22.875
that didn't make it, so,
obviously, not the great songs.

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:24.977
-I love that you went to Kinko's
and made it little --

00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:27.046
That's so cool.

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:28.847
And were they, like,
CDs you handed out

00:02:28.847 --> 00:02:30.850
at concerts or sold or...

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.953
-I think we sold them
out of our van or whatever.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:36.288
We were just trying to
make it, you know?

00:02:36.288 --> 00:02:37.723
We weren't even trying
to make it.

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:40.292
We just wanted the fans
that came to see us

00:02:40.292 --> 00:02:43.162
to have our music on a record,
because at that point,

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:45.231
"Just a Girl" --
we were playing that live

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:47.032
for over a year
before it ever came out.

00:02:47.032 --> 00:02:48.901
Like, we were playing it.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:51.037
-It was such a different sound

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:52.571
and something
I just had not heard.

00:02:52.571 --> 00:02:54.741
Like, your voice was
so different,

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:56.976
and, like, I go like,
"Oh, what is this?"

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.378
Like, it's like ska,
but, like, who is this singer,

00:02:59.378 --> 00:03:00.813
and what's going on?

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:02.681
And "Just a Girl,"
and you go like --

00:03:02.681 --> 00:03:05.517
I loved --
And cranking it so loud.

00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:07.520
It was just, like,
I mean, guys loved it.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.456
Everyone -- It's an anthem now.
-It's still crazy.

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:11.591
-Does it freak you out?

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:14.093
-I actually named my
Vegas residency --

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:17.931
I did that for about two years,
which was amazing,

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:19.765
and I named it
"The Just a Girl Show"

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:22.368
basically because
it felt, like, so right,

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.537
and the show was very much
reflective of, like,

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:26.572
kind of the moments in time

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:28.608
of all the
different versions of me.

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:31.110
And -- But I always thought
that that song

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.412
would kind of be outdated
in some way

00:03:33.412 --> 00:03:36.182
or I'd out grow it 'cause I'd
be, like, a woman or something,

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:38.684
and it still is so relevant.

00:03:38.684 --> 00:03:40.019
It's insane.
-Yeah.

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:41.153
-So proud of that.

00:03:41.153 --> 00:03:43.722
-Thank you for doing our
country bit earlier,

00:03:43.722 --> 00:03:46.425
which is kind of fun,
because, speaking of country,

00:03:46.425 --> 00:03:49.228
your song with Blake Shelton,
"Nobody But You,"

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:51.797
is up for a CMT Music Award.

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:53.232
You're basically --
-That's so crazy.

00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:56.268
-How does that -- That's got to
feel amazing, right?

00:03:56.268 --> 00:03:57.803
-It's really weird.

00:03:57.803 --> 00:04:00.973
It's just -- I've definitely
bounced, you know,

00:04:00.973 --> 00:04:03.075
in different genres
through my career,

00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:05.144
and it was just kind of easy.

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:07.046
Like, it was just natural.

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:12.051
And this was one I never would
ever imagine would happen.

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:13.786
Although, my parents grew up --

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:16.955
Like, I grew up on, like,
bluegrass music and, like, folk.

00:04:16.955 --> 00:04:19.925
So it's kind of similar
in a way.

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:22.061
But, like, the first concert
I ever went to was

00:04:22.061 --> 00:04:23.963
an Emmylou Harris concert
when I was, like,

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.331
in Girl Scouts or whatever.

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:30.402
So it's just crazy
to think that now I'm on

00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:33.306
two records, actually,
country records,

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:35.441
and on the radio, like,
country radio?

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:36.876
That's hard for me to believe.

00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:41.747
And -- But so honored,
and I love the song so much.

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:44.416
And I've learned so much
about country music.

00:04:44.416 --> 00:04:47.786
-Country fans don't really let
everyone into their world.

00:04:47.786 --> 00:04:49.755
You can't be a phony,
'cause they'll sniff you out

00:04:49.755 --> 00:04:51.090
and they'll be like --

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.291
-Yeah, that is true.

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:54.060
And that's one thing about
Blake Shelton.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.228
He is not a phony.

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:58.264
That guy is like Blake
no matter where you are.

00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:00.400
Like, I remember when
I first met him

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.301
and I took him to some, like,
Hollywood party.

00:05:02.301 --> 00:05:04.670
And I was like -- I didn't know
what he was going to be like.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.238
You know what I'm saying?
And he was just him.

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:08.708
Again, I bring him
to my parents' house, he's him.

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.309
Like, he doesn't care
that it's my dad.

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:11.744
Like, he's still Blake.

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:15.281
Like, he's so authentic, and
that's what I love about him.

00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:16.682
-Well, so are you.

00:05:16.682 --> 00:05:19.952
I mean, did you get to work on
any new music of your own?

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.120
-I have been.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:24.623
I never was, like,
expecting that I would,

00:05:24.623 --> 00:05:28.461
but I wrote one song,
and it was like a wildfire.

00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:30.696
Like, they just --
I think I have like over

00:05:30.696 --> 00:05:32.932
24 songs or something like that.

00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:35.368
I don't even know how,
'cause, sometimes, like,

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:37.770
you press that button,
and nothing happens.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.304
Like, the elevator is stuck.

00:05:39.304 --> 00:05:42.975
And this time was like -- It was
just, like, coming through,

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:45.310
and I was like,
"This is the greatest thing."

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.346
And it just feels so happy

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:49.982
and it's just so fun
to make new music,

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.484
'cause there is
really nothing else

00:05:51.484 --> 00:05:53.619
that makes me feel that way.

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:57.089
And this period, like, before it
comes out, is my favorite,

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:59.191
like, 'cause it's yours,
and you don't --

00:05:59.191 --> 00:06:01.727
Like, the anticipation,
like, the excitement --

00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:03.261
it's so big.

00:06:03.261 --> 00:06:06.432
-Yeah, no one else gets to have
any input on it yet.

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:07.866
Like, the world
hasn't seen it yet.

00:06:07.866 --> 00:06:09.301
You're like,
"This is so exciting.

00:06:09.301 --> 00:06:11.503
Like, no can --" Yeah, it's so
ex-- That is a good --

00:06:11.503 --> 00:06:13.339
-It's like, I make up
all these, like,

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:15.674
huge dreams and, like,
ideas that people

00:06:15.674 --> 00:06:19.077
are going to be so excited when
they hear it, like I am.

00:06:19.077 --> 00:06:21.680
And it's like a fantasy
that I get to live in

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.916
before it comes out.

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:25.250
And once the records
come out, like,

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.719
you kind of never, like,
listen to them again, you know?

00:06:27.719 --> 00:06:29.255
And during the demo phase,

00:06:29.255 --> 00:06:31.723
you're, like, listening
repeatedly and, like --

00:06:31.723 --> 00:06:34.227
It's like the lyrics are like --
It's like therapy.

00:06:34.227 --> 00:06:36.061
It's like they're healing you,
you know?

00:06:36.061 --> 00:06:37.629
-I can't wait for that
to come out.

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:38.964
Can you stick around?

00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:40.399
I just want to talk about
"The Voice" when we come back.

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:42.435
Is that alright?
-Please.

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:44.737
-More with Gwen Stefani when we
come back, everybody!

